# Final boundary review findings

This supplements the architecture review with findings from the actual populated buildspace. It does not record human acceptance.

| Boundary | Finding and resolution |
| --- | --- |
| Hidden layout to visible board | Initial splitter sizes were captured while a connected board had zero width. Defaults now measure the available size when the board becomes visible; the recorded regression changes from 90-pixel columns to the expected proportional layout. |
| Full node detail versus referenced summaries | A self-referencing type could replace its own complete editor record with a minimal relation summary. Detail records now remain authoritative, and global type/actor references remain usable without admitting a node from another buildspace. |
| Buildspace URL and display identity | The compact toolbar label comes from the owning contribution. URL selection is validated, survives reload and wins over stale local storage; invalid URLs provide a directory recovery path. Foreign node URLs cannot silently pair one board with another space's editor. |
| Review locks and regression fixtures | A selection-race test left a submitted review locking the shared fixture. The test now releases its own review in finally, and the later agent test checks prompt acceptance explicitly. The product lock invariant was preserved. |
| Annotation defaults and delayed capture | The source's buildspace and conversation are captured before asynchronous work. Known explicit conversation references outrank the currently open chat. Stacked items retain their own destinations; persisted links carry the destination space and never send a message automatically. |
| Paged data observation | Tests measuring cards wait for the requested card page, independently of inspector readiness. They retain the same behavioral assertions instead of mistaking a loading state for missing hierarchy. |
| Annotation evidence to agent input | Persisted annotation URLs resolve through the scoped owner reader at Send and on steering/follow-up. Comments, selected ranges and source identities are supplied as quoted evidence without a retrieval tool call. The canonical transcript preserves the original Markdown and explicit attachments; injected native images never leak into the stored user message. Cross-space references are rejected before execution. |
| Descendant progress and human attention | A new root/middle/leaf regression confirms that a descendant's substantive stage change updates ancestor timestamps and returns an acknowledged root to New. Attention-only changes do not create spurious ancestor updates. The existing owner behavior was correct; no product rewrite was necessary. |
| Provider control readiness | The provider selector appeared editable before its options arrived. It now exposes a disabled loading state, loads independently of unrelated settings requests, and prevents credential submission until its provider is resolved. A delayed-response test preserves the original wheel-selection assertion. |
| Settings save lifecycle | Save availability follows the selected topic and provider readiness. Late discovery cannot erase a newer successful-save message. A response for an earlier credential clears the draft only if both its provider and key still match; controlled races cover changed key text and a changed provider separately. |
| Review deep-link bootstrap | Overlapping startup reads could choose Node editor before learning that the selected node is a review. Automatic view selection now waits for hydration and never replaces an explicit user choice. A delayed-response regression covers both cases. |
| Preview feedback ownership and candidate identity | Managed Preview annotations resolve their owning review and default to that destination. The owner requires matching review, buildspace, captured session and immutable candidate key before writing. Preview creation and validation share the same key helper. Stacked feedback from an older submission is rejected without discarding the draft; historical discussions remain intact. |
| Test interception lifetime | Feed assertions completed while a mocked response was still being consumed. The fixture now drains pending handlers before closing the context. A deterministic before/after regression retains the original behavior assertions and does not ignore route errors. |
| Intake across refresh and retry | Pending actions and saved-item identity previously belonged to replaceable form closures. They now belong to the source buildspace, survive refreshes and reuse the saved item after a failed start. Completion preserves newer draft text and model choices. Both forced-refresh failures were reproduced before the fix; all eight navigation tests pass afterward, including duplicate-free retry. |
| Layout measurement lifetime | Complete-suite refreshes exposed a gap between checking that a card was visible and measuring its now-replaced DOM. Board and Preview measurements now locate the exact mounted target and capture geometry atomically. Readiness polling checks identity and mounting only; the existing geometry assertions still determine success. |
| Captured package scopes and cold owner builds | The first submitted candidate exposed a stale root dependency after its nested template dependency scope was omitted. Materialization now follows captured workspace and directory-file declarations recursively, validates package identity and link containment, and fails on broken installed copies. Manifest-only build discovery orders declared local owners before their consumers, rejects cycles and missing owners, and never schedules third-party package builds. Independent review covered both mechanisms; the withdrawn submission preserves the original failure. |

The populated annotation capture also exposed a separate scaling problem: 450 cards produced a 19 MB request and more than 14 seconds of preparation. Reusing actual stylesheet rules and retaining current form, scroll, visibility and custom-host state reduced the final payload to 300 KB. The editor appeared in 154 ms; capture plus the independent header geometry check completed in 1.3 seconds in the recorded fixture. Creating or stacking waits for the chosen capture, and controls cannot change the captured context during that operation.

The source and reconstructed screenshots were compared visually. A hidden popover/unslotted child initially shifted the global header; preserving composed visibility corrected it and an exact header-geometry regression now checks that boundary. Reconstruction remains scriptless and network-denied. Managed candidate Preview captures actual browser pixels; ordinary DOM reconstruction does not claim to reproduce every cross-origin or shadow-internal surface.

The recurring asynchronous UI boundary is explicit: capture the owning context and submitted values before awaiting work; keep pending/retry identity outside replaceable DOM; and clear a draft only when it still matches the submitted values. Readiness tests must observe the mounted view they intend to measure. These principles apply to node saves, intake, annotation capture/delivery and provider settings rather than relying on event timing or fixed sleeps.
